cache:query:What is the story of Cain, including his background as the son of Adam and Eve and the events involving his offering, his conflict with Abel, and the consequences described in Genesis?
$982
Cain was the first son of Adam and Eve, born after they “knew” one another (the first “seed” of the couple) [chunk-41]. He worked the soil as a “husbandman,” while his brother Abel tended sheep [chunk-41].  

When the time came to bring gifts to the Lord, Cain offered “the fruits of the earth” [chunk-42], whereas Abel offered “the firstlings of his flock” and their fat, which earned the Lord’s favorable regard [chunk-42].  

Because God favored Abel’s offering, tension arose between the brothers, and Cain killed Abel—“the seed for Abel, whom Cain slew” [chunk-54].  

The murder led to further developments in the family line: after Abel’s death, Eve later bore another son, Seth, whom she named “God hath given me another seed for Abel” [chunk-54].  

Thus, Genesis presents Cain as Adam and Eve’s first son, his agricultural offering that was rejected, his killing of Abel, and the subsequent birth of Seth as a replacement.
$4
